{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim side (intuition as 'compressed expertise') and its mechanism ('chunking,' 'atomization error') directly undercut its own counterconditions. Countercondition 1 concedes the expert loses on 'highly procedural and low-complexity' tasks — i.e., tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes where a checklist is most applicable — yet the target never establishes what scope of task it is defending the expert on. More critically, Countercondition 2 invokes 'known intuitive failure modes (e.g., availability heuristic)' under stress, but the core claim that intuition is 'compressed expertise' and the 'atomization error' mechanism assume the expert's pattern-recognition is reliably superior; they provide no account of when compressed expertise becomes a bias rather than accuracy, so the countercondition is a dangling concession that the central argument never reconciles. Per the problem, reliability must be argued 'on average' across tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, and the target fails to weigh its conceded procedural-task losses against its asserted complex-task gains, nor does it show the evidence that would distinguish genuine expertise from heuristic failure within a single evaluation.","counterexample":[{"task":"A procedural medical diagnosis task with a validated checklist (e.g., Well's criteria for DVT) has objectively verifiable outcomes; per countercondition 1 the panel should outperform, yet the claim asserts the expert 'remains more reliable' without averaging over the mixed task set."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}